  'discerning-false-teachers-south-africa': {
    title: 'How to Recognise a False Teacher: A South African Christian\'s Biblical Guide',
    date: '2026-07-30',
    excerpt: 'South Africa\'s religious marketplace is large, loud, and growing. Here is what the Bible actually says about how to recognise a false teacher, with practical questions every Sandton Christian can ask before committing to a ministry.',
    tags: ['discernment', 'false teaching', 'pastor', 'church', 'South Africa', 'Johannesburg', 'Bible study', 'Christian living', 'discipleship', 'warning'],
    content: `
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">South Africa has one of the most active religious marketplaces in the world. Most of these ministries are sincere. Most are trying to follow the Bible. But not all of them are. The Bible is clear that false teachers will come. The Bible is clear that the Christian is to be on guard. The Bible does not say the Christian is to be naive.</p>
      <h2>What the Bible Actually Says About False Teachers</h2>
      <p>Matthew 7:15-20 — <em>Watch out for false prophets. They come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ferocious wolves. By their fruit you will recognise them.</em> 1 John 4:1 — <em>Do not believe every spirit, but test the spirits to see whether they are from God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world.</em> 2 Timothy 4:3-4 — <em>The time will come when people will not put up with sound doctrine. Instead, to suit their own desires, they will gather around them a great number of teachers to say what their itching ears want to hear.</em> 2 Corinthians 11:13-15 — <em>Such people are false apostles, deceitful workers, masquerading as apostles of Christ. And no wonder, for Satan himself masquerades as an angel of light.</em> 2 Peter 2:1-3 — <em>There will be false teachers among you. They will secretly introduce destructive heresies. In their greed these teachers will exploit you with fabricated stories.</em> Galatians 1:8-9 — <em>If anybody is preaching to you a gospel other than what you accepted, let them be under God's curse!</em> Acts 17:11 — The Berean Jews <em>examined the Scriptures every day to see if what Paul said was true.</em> 1 Thessalonians 5:21 — <em>Test everything. Hold on to what is good.</em></p>
      <h2>The Seven Biblical Markers of a False Teacher</h2>
      <h3>Marker 1 — What Is the Gospel?</h3>
      <p>Is the gospel the historic Christian gospel — that Jesus Christ is the Son of God, that he died for our sins, that he was raised on the third day, that salvation is by grace through faith? Or is the gospel a different gospel — a gospel of personal breakthrough, of national blessing, of generational wealth? Galatians 1:8-9 is severe about this marker.</p>
      <h3>Marker 2 — What Is Said About Money?</h3>
      <p>Is the Bible's teaching on money — stewardship, generosity, contentment, the danger of the love of money — being taught? Or is it being replaced by the teaching that the Christian is to be rich? 2 Peter 2:3 is specific. <em>In their greed these teachers will exploit you with fabricated stories.</em></p>
      <h3>Marker 3 — How Is the Leader Living?</h3>
      <p>Is the leader's family in order? Is the leader's money in order? Is the leader's ego in order? Is the leader approachable? Is the leader accountable to anyone? Matthew 7 — <em>By their fruit you will recognise them.</em> The fruit is the long-term fruit, not the conference fruit.</p>
      <h3>Marker 4 — Is the Bible Actually Preached?</h3>
      <p>Is the Bible actually being preached? Or is the Bible being replaced by personal revelation, by prophetic words, by stories about what God told the leader? Acts 17:11 — the noble thing is to examine the Scriptures daily. A ministry that does not even own the Bible as the authority is a ministry that has failed this marker.</p>
      <h3>Marker 5 — How Are the Vulnerable Treated?</h3>
      <p>How does the ministry treat the widows, the orphans, the divorced, the unemployed? James 1:27 — <em>Religion that God our Father accepts as pure and faultless is this: to look after orphans and widows in their distress.</em> The marker is whether the vulnerable are the central concern of the ministry, or the marketing of the ministry.</p>
      <h3>Marker 6 — Is There Accountability?</h3>
      <p>Is the leader accountable to anyone? Is there a board? Is there an elder team? Is there a denomination? Hebrews 13:17 — <em>Have confidence in your leaders and submit to their authority, because they keep watch over you as those who must give an account.</em> A ministry where the leader is the final word on everything has set up a structure the Bible does not endorse.</p>
      <h3>Marker 7 — What Happens to the Questioner?</h3>
      <p>What happens to the Christian who asks a hard question? What happens to the Christian who says <em>show me in the Bible</em>? A ministry that responds by shaming the questioner, by labelling the questioner as having a spirit of criticism, by isolating the questioner — that ministry has failed this marker.</p>
      <h2>What the Bible Does Not Require</h2>
      <p>The Bible does not require the Christian to find a perfect ministry. There is no perfect ministry. The Bible does not require the Christian to stay in a ministry that has clearly failed the seven markers. The Bible does not require the Christian to be suspicious of every ministry. Most ministries deserve the benefit of the doubt. The seven markers are for the new ministry the Christian is considering. The seven markers are for the ministry that has raised a question.</p>
      <h2>A Prayer for the Discerning South African Christian</h2>
      <p><em>God of the truth — we thank you for your word. We thank you for the Berean Jews who examined the Scriptures daily. We thank you for the Spirit who guides us into all truth. We pray for discernment. We pray for the wisdom to test what is being taught. We pray for the courage to ask the hard question. We pray for the humility to leave when the Bible says leave. We pray for the grace to stay when the Bible says stay. We pray for the ministries that are faithfully preaching your word. We pray for the leaders who are accountable to you and to your people. We pray for the ones who are caught in the false ministries, who do not yet know they are caught. We pray that they will read the Bible before they sign the form. We pray that they will test the fruit before they send the money. Give us the wisdom of the serpent and the innocence of the dove (Matthew 10:16). Give us the courage of the Bereans. In the name of the one who is the way, the truth, and the life. Amen.</em></p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-8"><em>Johannesburg Bible Study Church meets online every Wednesday at 7:30pm. We test everything. We hold on to what is good. Call +27 77 487 1295 or +27 67 442 4461 to join.</em></p>
    `,
  },
  'intergenerational-discipleship-south-africa': {
    title: 'Passing the Faith to the Next Generation: A Biblical Model for South African Families and Churches',
    date: '2026-07-30',
    excerpt: 'South African culture has always been intergenerational. The Bible has a great deal to say about older and younger believers learning from each other. Here is what Titus 2 actually teaches, and how Sandton Bible study members are practising it.',
    tags: ['discipleship', 'mentoring', 'older women', 'older men', 'younger women', 'younger men', 'South Africa', 'Sandton', 'Johannesburg', 'Bible study', 'community', 'family'],
    content: `
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">There is a question that almost every Christian grandparent in Johannesburg carries. It is the question the grandparent asks at the kitchen table when the grandchild is on the iPad in the next room. <em>How do I pass my faith to a generation that does not seem to need it from me?</em></p>
      <p>South African culture has always been more intergenerational than most other cultures. The Ubuntu principle — <em>I am because we are</em> — assumes that the older and the younger are part of the same thing. The Bible study that meets on a Wednesday evening in Sandton includes grandmothers in their seventies and young adults in their twenties and teenagers in their middle years — all sitting around the same table.</p>
      <h2>What the Bible Actually Says About the Older and the Younger</h2>
      <p>Titus 2:1-8 — <em>Teach the older men to be temperate, worthy of respect, self-controlled, and sound in faith, in love and in endurance. Likewise, teach the older women to be reverent in the way they live. Then they can urge the younger women to love their husbands and children. Similarly, teach the younger men to be self-controlled. In everything set them an example by doing what is good.</em> 2 Timothy 2:2 — <em>Entrust to reliable people who will also be qualified to teach others.</em> Deuteronomy 6:6-7 — <em>Impress them on your children. Talk about them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road.</em> Psalm 71:18 — <em>Even when I am old and grey, do not forsake me, my God, till I declare your power to the next generation.</em> 1 Timothy 5:1-2 — <em>Treat older women as mothers, and younger women as sisters, with absolute purity.</em> 1 Peter 5:5 — <em>You younger men, submit yourselves to your elders. All of you, clothe yourselves with humility toward one another.</em></p>
      <p>What you notice is that the Bible does not present the older and the younger as separate ministries. The Bible presents them as one body that needs the other.</p>
      <h2>The Five Roles the Bible Describes</h2>
      <h3>Role 1 — The Older Woman Is to Teach the Younger Woman</h3>
      <p>Titus 2:3-5. The teaching is not the classroom teaching. The teaching is the kitchen-table teaching. The teaching is the <em>how to love your husband and children</em> teaching. The friendship is the kind where the younger woman can ask the older woman the question she would not ask her own mother.</p>
      <h3>Role 2 — The Older Man Is to Be the Example to the Younger Man</h3>
      <p>Titus 2:6-8. The older man is not to lecture the younger man. The older man is to be the example. The younger man is to watch how the older man treats his wife. The younger man is to watch how the older man handles the money. The example is more important than the lecture.</p>
      <h3>Role 3 — The Older Christian Is to Declare the Power of God to the Next Generation</h3>
      <p>Psalm 71:18. The older Christian is the one who has seen the most. The older Christian is the one who can testify to the faithfulness of God across the decades. The younger Christian needs this testimony.</p>
      <h3>Role 4 — The Younger Christian Is to Submit to the Older Christian</h3>
      <p>1 Peter 5:5. The submission is not inferiority. The submission is the posture that allows the older Christian's wisdom to land.</p>
      <h3>Role 5 — The Older and Younger Are to Honour Each Other as Family</h3>
      <p>1 Timothy 5:1-2. The older man is to be honoured as a father. The older woman as a mother. The younger man as a brother. The younger woman as a sister.</p>
      <h2>What the South African Intergenerational Bible Study Looks Like in Practice</h2>
      <h3>Practice 1 — The Shared Table</h3>
      <p>The Wednesday Bible study is held at a table that can seat twelve. The table regularly includes the grandmother in her seventies and the university student in his early twenties. The shared table is the intergenerational thing. The shared table is what Titus 2 assumes.</p>
      <h3>Practice 2 — The Reading of the Same Passage</h3>
      <p>The same passage is read out loud by whoever volunteers. The grandmother reads. The university student reads. The passage is read in the same room. The passage is read at the same pace. The passage is the same passage for everyone.</p>
      <h3>Practice 3 — The Two Questions the Older Ask and the Younger Ask</h3>
      <p>The older Christian asks the first question — the long view. <em>Where have you seen this passage in your life? What did this passage mean to you when you were young?</em> The younger Christian asks the second question — the immediate. <em>What does this passage mean for me this week?</em> The two questions together cover the arc of the Christian life.</p>
      <h3>Practice 4 — The Story of the Older Christian</h3>
      <p>Several times a year, the older Christian in the Bible study is invited to share the testimony. The testimony is not the altar-call testimony. The testimony is the long testimony. The testimony is the inheritance.</p>
      <h3>Practice 5 — The Practical Service Between the Generations</h3>
      <p>The younger Christian with the car drives the older Christian to the doctor's appointment. The older Christian with the experience helps the younger Christian write the CV. The intergenerational service is the Titus 2 thing in action.</p>
      <h3>Practice 6 — The Holiday Together</h3>
      <p>Once a year, several of the Bible study families go away together for a weekend. The weekend is the family. The weekend is the place where the grandmother tells the story of 1985 and the university student tells the story of the TikTok they saw last week.</p>
      <h2>The Hard Truths About the Intergenerational Christian Community</h2>
      <h3>The Hard Truth 1 — The older and younger do not always understand each other.</h3>
      <p>The grandmother does not always understand the young professional's decision to move back home at 28. The young professional does not always understand the grandmother's decision to stay in the marriage for 50 years. The two do not have to agree. The two have to listen.</p>
      <h3>The Hard Truth 2 — The intergenerational community takes time.</h3>
      <p>The intergenerational community is not built in a programme. The intergenerational community is built over years. The intergenerational community is the slowest of the slow obediences.</p>
      <h3>The Hard Truth 3 — The intergenerational community requires the older Christian to admit weakness.</h3>
      <p>The older Christian who is willing to admit the weakness is the older Christian the younger Christian will trust.</p>
      <h3>The Hard Truth 4 — The intergenerational community requires the younger Christian to ask.</h3>
      <p>The younger Christian who is too proud to ask the older Christian is the younger Christian who will go through the thing alone. The asking is the submission to the elder.</p>
      <h2>A Prayer for the South African Christian Family Across the Generations</h2>
      <p><em>God of the generations — we thank you for the grandmother who taught us to pray. We thank you for the grandfather who read us the Bible stories. We thank you for the parent who drove us to the church. We thank you for the older Christian at the Wednesday Bible study who tells us what the faith looked like in 1985. We thank you for the university student who asks the question we have been afraid to ask. We thank you for the young professional who keeps showing up on a Wednesday evening. We thank you for the teenager who sits at the table even though the conversation is sometimes over their head. We confess that we have separated the generations. We confess that we have built churches for the young and the old separately. Help us to do the slow work. Help us to put the table in the room where both can sit. Help us to read the passage at the pace that both can hear. Help us to ask the long-view question and the immediate-view question. Help us to share the testimony. Help us to drive each other to the appointment. Help us to pray for each other through the night. In the name of the one who was a child in the temple, a young man in the carpenter's shop, a teacher in the synagogue, an older man on the cross. Amen.</em></p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-8"><em>Johannesburg Bible Study Church meets online every Wednesday at 7:30pm. The shared table is the intergenerational thing. The Bible is read at the pace both can hear. Call +27 77 487 1295 or +27 67 442 4461 to join.</em></p>
    `,
  },

