      <p>The Christian who is looking for a community is encouraged to visit two or three different communities before deciding. The Christian is encouraged to ask questions. The Christian is encouraged to take notes. The Christian is encouraged to give the community at least three visits before deciding to stay or leave.</p>

      <h3>Fifth Step — Be Patient With Yourself and With the Community</h3>
      <p>Finding a Christian community takes time. Trusting a new community takes time. Allowing the new community to know the real you takes time. The Christian is encouraged to be patient. The Christian is encouraged not to give up after one bad experience. The Christian is encouraged to keep looking, keep asking, keep praying, and keep showing up.</p>

      <h2>A Closing Word to the Christian Who Has Been Hurt by a Community</h2>
      <p>If you have been hurt by a Christian community before — if you have been pressured, controlled, shamed, or exploited — this section is for you. The Bible is clear that not every Christian community is healthy. The Bible is clear that some Christian communities have failed. The Bible is clear that the Christian who has been hurt by a community is not the Christian who has been failed by God.</p>
      <p><strong>Psalm 147:3</strong> — <em>"He heals the brokenhearted and binds up their wounds."</em></p>
      <p><strong>Matthew 11:28</strong> — <em>"Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest."</em></p>
      <p><strong>Romans 8:1</strong> — <em>"Therefore, there is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus."</em></p>
      <p>The Christian who has been hurt is invited to come to Jesus, not away from Jesus. The Christian who has been hurt is invited to find a Bible-based community, not to give up on community altogether. The Christian who has been hurt is invited to take the risk of trusting a community again, slowly, with wisdom, with patience, and with the help of God.</p>

      <h2>A Closing Word to the Christian Who Is Looking Tonight</h2>
      <p>If you are reading this tonight, alone, with no Christian community, with no Bible study, with no one to call — the Bible is for you. The Bible is the Christian's first community. The Bible is the Christian's first friend. The Bible is the place where the Christian can hear the voice of God until they find the people of God.</p>
      <p>Our Wednesday Bible study in Parkmore is online. We meet every Wednesday at 7:30pm. We do not require commitment. We do not require tithes. We do not require anything from you other than a willingness to show up. If you would like to join, call Mr. Sim on +27 77 487 1295 or Ms. Dora on +27 67 442 4461, and we will send you the link. If the WiFi is out, we will move to WhatsApp. If the WhatsApp is out, we will meet in Parkmore. The Wednesday night is the Wednesday night, with or without WiFi.</p>
      <p>You are not alone. The Bible is with you. The body of Christ is with you. And the God who makes the body is waiting for you to find your place in it.</p>

