      <h3>4. 1 Corinthians 14:33 — God Not a God of Disorder but of Peace</h3>
      <p><em>"For God is not a God of disorder but of peace — as in all the congregations of the Lord's people."</em></p>
      <p>The Bible does not endorse disorder. The Bible does not endorse chaos. The Bible does not endorse the kind of disorder that prevents people from hearing the word. The Bible calls the Christian community to order, to peace, to clarity, to a way of meeting that allows the word to be heard.</p>

      <h2>What the Small Bible Study Does Well</h2>

      <h3>1. The Small Bible Study Knows You</h3>
      <p>The Christian in the small Bible study is known. The Christian is known by name. The Christian is known by their story. The Christian is known when they are absent. The Christian is known when they are struggling. The Christian is known when they are celebrating. The small Bible study is the kind of community where, if you are not there for two weeks, the others will phone you.</p>

      <h3>2. The Small Bible Study Reads the Bible Slowly</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study can read the Bible slowly. The small Bible study can read one verse at a time. The small Bible study can read a chapter and discuss it for two hours. The small Bible study can read the Bible in the original languages. The small Bible study is the kind of community where the Christian can ask the hard question without feeling rushed.</p>

      <h3>3. The Small Bible Study Adapts to the Members</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study can adapt to the members. The small Bible study can move to WhatsApp when the WiFi is out. The small Bible study can move to a different suburb when the load-shedding hits. The small Bible study can add a new member without changing the format. The small Bible study is flexible in a way that the megachurch often cannot be.</p>

      <h3>4. The Small Bible Study Is Honest About Suffering</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study is honest about suffering. The small Bible study can name the suffering of the members. The small Bible study can weep with the weeping. The small Bible study can sit with the suffering. The small Bible study can be the kind of community where the Christian can say, <em>I am not okay,</em> and the others can respond with prayer and practical help.</p>

      <h2>What the Small Bible Study Often Gets Wrong</h2>

      <h3>1. The Small Bible Study Can Become Cliquish</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study that has been meeting for ten years can become an in-group. The small Bible study can become a place where the new person feels like an outsider. The small Bible study can become a place where the new person is welcomed but not really included. The small Bible study that has been meeting for ten years needs to be intentional about welcoming the new people in.</p>

      <h3>3. The Small Bible Study Can Become Over-Attached to the Leader</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study that has been meeting for ten years can become over-attached to the leader. The small Bible study can become a place where the leader's opinion is the only opinion. The small Bible study can become a place where the members are afraid to disagree with the leader. The small Bible study needs to be intentional about not putting the leader on a pedestal.</p>

      <h3>3. The Small Bible Study Can Lack the Resources for Outreach</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study often does not have the budget, the building, the band, the sound system, the children's ministry, or the youth ministry that the megachurch has. The small Bible study often cannot host the kind of outreach event that the megachurch can. The small Bible study often cannot invite the unbeliever who is looking for the perfect worship service. The small Bible study needs to be honest about what it can and cannot do.</p>

      <h3>4. The Small Bible Study Can Become Spiritually Stagnant</h3>
      <p>The small Bible study that has been meeting for ten years can become a place where the members have heard the same teaching, the same prayers, the same songs, and the same stories so many times that they have stopped growing. The small Bible study needs to be intentional about reading new books, inviting new teachers, exploring new passages, and reaching out to new people.</p>

      <h2>What the Megachurch Does Well</h2>

      <h3>1. The Megachurch Provides Resources for Outreach</h3>
      <p>The megachurch has the budget, the building, the band, the sound system, the children's ministry, the youth ministry, the counselling ministry, the marriage ministry, the divorce recovery ministry, the financial counselling ministry, the recovery ministry, the missions ministry, and the outreach ministry that the small Bible study does not have. The megachurch can reach the people who would never come to a small Bible study.</p>

      <h3>2. The Megachurch Provides Quality Worship</h3>
      <p>The megachurch has the band, the singers, the sound engineers, the lighting engineers, the cameras, the screens, and the production team that can provide a high-quality worship experience. The megachurch can sing the songs that the small Bible study cannot sing. The megachurch can host the kind of worship that the small Bible study cannot host.</p>

      <h3>3. The Megachurch Provides a Sense of Belonging</h3>
      <p>The megachurch provides a sense of belonging. The Christian in the megachurch is part of a large community. The Christian in the megachurch is part of a community that is larger than themselves. The Christian in the megachurch is part of a community that has the same beliefs, the same values, and the same vision.</p>

      <h2>What the Megachurch Often Gets Wrong</h2>

      <h3>1. The Megachurch Can Become Performance-Driven</h3>
      <p>The megachurch can become a place where the worship is a performance. The megachurch can become a place where the sermon is a performance. The megachurch can become a place where the leader is a celebrity. The megachurch can become a place where the Christian is a consumer of religious content rather than a participant in Christian community.</p>

      <h3>2. The Megachurch Can Become Top-Down</h3>
      <p>The megachurch can become a place where the leader's vision is the only vision. The megachurch can become a place where the members have no say. The megachurch can become a place where the leader is the only voice. The megachurch can become a place where the Christian is told what to believe, what to give, and how to live.</p>

      <h3>3. The Megachurch Can Become Anonymous</h3>
      <p>The megachurch can become a place where the Christian is anonymous. The megachurch can become a place where the Christian is one of three thousand. The megachurch can become a place where the Christian is unknown. The megachurch can become a place where the Christian can attend for ten years without anyone knowing their story.</p>

      <h3>4. The Megachurch Can Become Dependent on the Leader</h3>
      <p>The megachurch can become a place where the leader is irreplaceable. The megachurch can become a place where the leader is the reason the Christian attends. The megachurch can become a place where the leader is the centre of gravity. The megachurch can become a place where, when the leader leaves, the church collapses.</p>

      <h2>How a Christian in Johannesburg Can Find Their Place Between the Two</h2>
      <p>After ten years of running a Wednesday Bible study in Parkmore, we have learned that the Christian in Johannesburg who finds their place is the Christian who is honest about themselves.</p>

      <h3>1. Be Honest About What You Need</h3>
      <p>The Christian who needs the small Bible study should find the small Bible study. The Christian who needs the megachurch should find the megachurch. The Christian who needs both should find both. The Christian is encouraged to be honest about what they need.</p>

      <h3>2. Do Not Judge Others for Their Choice</h3>
      <p>The Christian who attends the small Bible study should not judge the Christian who attends the megachurch. The Christian who attends the megachurch should not judge the Christian who attends the small Bible study. The Bible does not command one over the other.</p>

      <h3>3. Look for Health, Not Size</h3>
      <p>The Christian in Johannesburg is encouraged to look for a healthy community, not a large one. The Christian is encouraged to look for a community where the Bible is taught, where prayer is offered, where confession is honoured, where service is expected, where the leader is accountable, and where the members are loved.</p>

      <h3>4. Be Willing to Move Between the Two</h3>
      <p>The Christian in Johannesburg is encouraged to be willing to move between the two. The Christian can attend the megachurch on Sunday morning and the small Bible study on Wednesday evening. The Christian can attend the small Bible study for ten years and then try the megachurch for six months. The Christian is encouraged to be flexible.</p>

      <h3>5. Build the Community You Wish Existed</h3>
      <p>The Christian in Johannesburg who cannot find the community they wish existed is invited to build it. The Christian can invite four other Christians to meet in their living room. The Christian can host a Bible study. The Christian can start a small group. The Christian can plant a church. The Christian is encouraged to build the community they wish existed.</p>

      <h2>A Closing Word to the Christian Who Has Been Hurt by Either Extreme</h2>
      <p>If you have been hurt by a small Bible study — if the small Bible study was cliquish, gossipy, or spiritually stagnant — the Bible is for you. If you have been hurt by a megachurch — if the megachurch was performative, top-down, or anonymous — the Bible is for you. The Bible is the source of the cure for both extremes.</p>
      <p><strong>Psalm 133:1</strong> — <em>"How good and pleasant it is when God's people live together in unity!"</em></p>
      <p><strong>Hebrews 10:24-25</strong> — <em>"And let us consider how we may spur one another on toward love and good deeds, not giving up meeting together, as some are in the habit of doing, but encouraging one another."</em></p>
      <p>The Christian who has been hurt is invited to keep going. The Christian who has been hurt is invited to find a healthy community — small or large, depending on what the Christian needs. The Christian who has been hurt is invited to keep reading the Bible, keep praying, keep showing up, keep trusting that God has a community for them.</p>

      <h2>A Closing Word to the Christian Who Has Been Longing for the Small Bible Study</h2>
      <p>If you have been longing for the small Bible study — if you have been longing for the kind of community where four or six people know each other by name, where the Bible is read slowly, where the prayers are honest, where the leader is approachable, where the members are accountable — our Wednesday Bible study in Parkmore is here. We meet online every Wednesday at 7:30pm. We do not require commitment. We do not require tithes. We do not require anything from you other than a willingness to show up. If you would like to join, call Mr. Sim on +27 77 487 1295 or Ms. Dora on +27 67 442 4461, and we will send you the link. If the WiFi is out, we will move to WhatsApp. If the WhatsApp is out, we will meet in Parkmore. The Wednesday night is the Wednesday night, with or without WiFi.</p>
      <p>You are not alone. The Bible is with you. The body of Christ is with you. And the God who makes the body is waiting for you to find your place in it.</p>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-8"><em>Johannesburg Bible Study Church meets online every Wednesday at 7:30pm for a small Bible study with the heart of a megachurch — or, perhaps more accurately, with the heart of the early church. The Bible study is free, open to anyone, and free to join or to leave. Call Mr. Sim on +27 77 487 1295 or Ms. Dora on +27 67 442 4461 to join.</em></p>
    `,
  },
};

// Hymn region images
const hymnRegionImages: Record<string, string> = {
  'English': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1509099836639-18ba1795216d?w=1200&q=80',
  'Zulu': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516026672322-bc52d61a55d5?w=1200&q=80',
  'Xhosa': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1523803832-1c1b5e8ed3c9?w=1200&q=80',
  'Swahili': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1489392191049-fc10c97e64b6?w=1200&q=80',
  'Dinka': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1489424731084-a5d8b219a5bb?w=1200&q=80',
  'Shona': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517783999520-f068a47e5f53?w=1200&q=80',
  'Korean': 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534008757030-27299c4371b6?w=1200&q=80',
};

const post = posts[slug];

if (!post) {
  return Astro.redirect('/blog');
}

const schema = getBlogPostSchema(post.title, post.excerpt, post.date, 'Johannesburg Bible Study Church');

// Get image for hymn
const isHymn = 'isHymn' in post && post.isHymn;
const language = 'language' in post ? post.language as string : '';
const heroImage = isHymn && language ? (hymnRegionImages[language] || hymnRegionImages['English']) : null;
// Featured post hero image for new South Africa localised posts
const postHeroImage = 'heroImage' in post ? (post.heroImage as string) : null;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={post.title}
  description={post.excerpt}
  ogType="article"
  publishDate={post.date}
  schema={schema}
>
  {isHymn && heroImage ? (
    // Hymn Hero Layout
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 z-0">
        <img 
          src={heroImage}
          alt={`${language} worship`}
          class="w-full h-full object-cover"
        />
        <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-r from-slate-900/95 via-slate-900/80 to-slate-900/60"></div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- African Pattern Overlay -->
      <div class="absolute inset-0 z-10 opacity-5" style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22 viewBox=%220 0 80 80%22><path fill=%22%23f4e4bc%22 d=%22M0 0h40v40H0zM40 40h40v40H40z%22/><path fill=%22%23c9a227%22 d=%22M20 20h20v20H20z%22/></svg>'); background-size: 40px 40px;"></div>
      
      <div class="relative z-20 section-padding py-20 md:py-28">
        <div class="container-max">
          <div class="max-w-3xl">
            <!-- Breadcrumb -->
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-amber-200/80 text-sm mb-6">
              <a href="/" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Home</a>
              <span>/</span>
              <a href="/hymns" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Hymns</a>
              <span>/</span>
              <span class="text-white">{post.title}</span>
            </div>
            
            <!-- Language Badge -->
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
              <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 bg-amber-500/90 backdrop-blur-sm text-white px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full text-sm font-semibold shadow-lg">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M3 5h12M9 3v2m1.048 9.5A18.022 18.022 0 016.412 9m6.088 9h7M11 21l5-10 5 10M12.751 5C11.783 10.77 8.07 15.61 3 18.129" />
                </svg>
                {language}
              </span>
              {post.tags.filter(t => t !== 'hymn' && t !== language.toLowerCase()).map((tag) => (
                <span class="text-xs bg-white/20 backdrop-blur-sm text-white px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">{tag}</span>
              ))}
            </div>
            
            <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-bold text-white mb-6 leading-tight">
              {post.title}
            </h1>
            
            <p class="text-xl text-slate-200 mb-8 leading-relaxed">{post.excerpt}</p>
            
            <!-- Music Info Cards -->
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 mb-8">
              <div class="bg-white/10 backdrop-blur-md rounded-xl px-6 py-4 border border-white/20">
                <p class="text-xs text-amber-300 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Key</p>
                <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">{post.key}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white/10 backdrop-blur-md rounded-xl px-6 py-4 border border-white/20">
                <p class="text-xs text-amber-300 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Chords</p>
                <p class="text-lg font-mono text-white">{post.chords}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            
            <!-- Download Button -->
            <a
              href={`/hymns/${slug}.docx`}
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 bg-green-600 hover:bg-green-500 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl text-lg font-semibold transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-xl"
              download
            >
              <svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 10v6m0 0l-3-3m3 3l3-3m2 8H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2h5.586a1 1 0 01.707.293l5.414 5.414a1 1 0 01.293.707V19a2 2 0 01-2 2z" />
              </svg>
              Download Chord Sheet
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Wave Divider -->
      <div class="absolute bottom-0 left-0 right-0 z-20">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1440 100" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-full h-auto">
          <path d="M0 100L60 90C120 80 240 60 360 50C480 40 600 40 720 45C840 50 960 60 1080 65C1200 70 1320 70 1380 70L1440 70V100H0Z" fill="white"/>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </section>
    
    <!-- Lyrics Section -->
    <section class="section-padding">
      <div class="container-max max-w-4xl">
        <div class="prose prose-xl max-w-none text-gray-700" set:html={post.content} />
        
        <!-- Contribution CTA -->
        <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-50 to-orange-50 rounded-2xl border border-amber-100">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 bg-amber-100 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center">
              <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M4.318 6.318a4.5 4.5 0 000 6.364L12 20.364l7.682-7.682a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364-6.364L12 7.636l-1.318-1.318a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364 0z" />
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-1">Share Your Worship</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mb-3">Do you have lyrics or chord sheets to contribute? We'd love to add more songs from our community.</p>
              <a href="/contact" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-amber-600 hover:text-amber-700 font-medium text-sm">
                Contact Us →
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  ) : (
    // Regular Blog Post Layout
    <article class="section-padding">
      <div class="container-max max-w-3xl">
        <a href="/blog" class="text-sm text-blue-600 hover:underline mb-6 inline-block">
          ← Back to Blog
        </a>
        {postHeroImage && (
          <div class="mb-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={postHeroImage}
              alt={post.title}
              class="w-full h-64 md:h-96 object-cover"
              loading="eager"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
        )}
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">{post.date}</p>
        <h1 class="heading-1 mb-6">{post.title}</h1>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-8">
          {post.tags.map((tag) => (
            <span class="text-xs bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">{tag}</span>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700" set:html={post.content} />
      </div>
    </article>
  )}

  <!-- Share + Comments (only for non-hymn posts) -->
  {!isHymn && (
    <section class="section-tighter border-t border-gray-200">
      <div class="container-max max-w-3xl">
        <Share title={post.title} align="left" />
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  {!isHymn && <Comments pageSlug={`blog/${slug}`} pageTitle={post.title} topic="community" />}

  <section class="section-padding bg-gray-50">
    <div class="container-max max-w-3xl">
      <a href={isHymn ? '/hymns' : '/blog'} class="btn-secondary">
        ← {isHymn ? 'Back to All Hymns' : 'Back to Blog'}
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
